  "context_pill": "Income Tax Act, 1961 / Rebate u/s 87A / Old Tax Regime",
  "example_summary": "Tax liability computation for Mr. Manish (Resident, Age 47) under Old Tax Regime with Total Income of ₹ 4,15,000 for A.Y. 2026-27 resulting in Nil tax payable.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Summary Matrix: Old Tax Regime (Resident Individual < 60 Years)\n\n| Statutory Provision | Condition / Threshold | Tax Rate / Rebate Limit | Net Tax Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Basic Exemption Limit** | Income up to ₹ 2,50,000 | **Nil** | No Tax Liability |\n| **Slab 1 (5% Band)** | Income between ₹ 2,50,001 to ₹ 5,00,000 | **5%** of income exceeding ₹ 2,50,000 | Tax payable before Rebate |\n| **Section 87A Rebate** | Resident Individual with Total Income $\\le$ ₹ 5,00,000 | Lower of **Tax Payable** or **₹ 12,500** | Net Tax Liability becomes **Nil** |\n\n\n### Core Statutory Conditions Checklist\n\n- **Option to Opt Out**: Assessee must specifically exercise option to shift out of the default tax regime to claim old regime slab rates.\n- **Eligibility for Section 87A**: Available **only to Resident Individuals**. Non-residents and non-individual entities are strictly ineligible.\n- **Monetary Ceiling**: Total Income must not exceed **₹ 5,00,000** after all deductions under Chapter VI-A.\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Statutory Benchmark: Section 87A Limit\n> Under the Old Tax Regime, if Total Income crosses ₹ 5,00,000 even by Re. 1, the benefit of Section 87A rebate is completely lost, and marginal relief is NOT available under the old regime for Section 87A.",
